      question: 'What is the Isnad al-Majazi (metaphorical attribution) in Arabic rhetoric?',
      answer: 'Isnad al-Majazi (also called Majaz Aqli or Majaz Isnad) is attributing an action to something other than its actual agent, for rhetorical purposes. Types: (1) Attributing the action of the agent to the time in which it occurred: نَهَارُهُ صَائِمٌ وَلَيلُهُ قَائِمٌ (His day fasts and his night stands in prayer — the person fasts and prays, not the day and night). (2) Attributing the action to the place: نَهرٌ جَارٍ (a flowing river — the water flows, not the river). (3) Attributing the action to the cause: بَنَى الأَمِيرُ المَدِينَةَ (The amir built the city — he ordered it; the workers built it). The qarinah (indicator) that prevents the literal interpretation must be present. This phenomenon is extremely common in Arabic and in the Quran and requires recognition to avoid misinterpretation.',
