      answer: 'Dalil al-Iltizam is the evidence that a ruling is necessitated by another established ruling — what must logically follow from a ruling even if not explicitly stated. It is related to the concept of Iltizam in semantics (necessary implication). Example: if a ruling establishes that a wife is entitled to nafaqah (maintenance), this necessarily implies (yaltazimu) that the husband must provide housing, food, and clothing — even if each is not explicitly enumerated. Another example: the obligation of Salah necessarily implies the obligation of purification (taharah) — "la salata illa bil-tahur" (no prayer without purification). This type of evidence is distinguished from explicit textual evidence (mantiq al-nass) and from analogical evidence (mafhum al-mukhalafah).',
      source: 'Al-Ihkam fi Usul al-Ahkam, al-Amidi; Al-Mustasfa, al-Ghazali',
