      answer: 'Khabar al-Wahid (solitary report) is a hadith transmitted by fewer narrators than mutawatir, producing probable knowledge (zann) rather than certainty. The question of whether it can abrogate the Quran: (1) The majority of Shafiis and Hanbalis: yes, a sahih ahad hadith can specify or restrict a Quranic ruling (takhsis), and some hold it can even abrogate in limited cases — since the Prophet had the authority to legislate and abrogate. (2) The Hanafi school: ahad cannot abrogate a definitive Quranic ruling because the Quran is qati al-thabut while ahad is zanni — the certain cannot be overridden by the probable. (3) The Maliki school: takes a middle position. The underlying principle: does the Sunnah have independent legislative authority alongside the Quran, or is it subordinate? All agree the Sunnah explains and clarifies the Quran; the disagreement is whether it can override a clear Quranic text.',
      source: 'Al-Risalah, al-Shafii; Usul al-Sarakhsi; Al-Ihkam, al-Amidi',
      tags: ['khabar ahad', 'naskh', 'quran', 'advanced'],
