      question: 'What is the مَصْدَر المِيمِي (masdar mimi — mim masdar) and how does it differ from the standard masdar?',
      answer: 'The Masdar al-Mimi is a masdar formed on the patterns مَفْعَل or مَفْعِل (with a prefixed mim), used for the meaning of the verbal noun like the standard masdar but with slight nuances. Formation: From Form I verbs — مَذْهَبٌ (madhab — from ذَهَبَ), مَرْجِعٌ (marjiʿ — from رَجَعَ), مَوْقِفٌ (mawqif — from وَقَفَ), مَنْطِقٌ (mantiq — from نَطَقَ), مَعْرِفَةٌ (maʿrifah — from عَرَفَ). Note: the masdar mimi, ism al-makan, and ism al-zaman share the same patterns (مَفْعَل/مَفْعِل) — context distinguishes their function. From augmented verbs: same as ism al-maful form: تَعَلَّمَ → مُتَعَلَّمٌ (also functions as masdar mimi). The masdar mimi is widely used in Islamic terminology: مَنْهَجٌ (manhaj — methodology), مَذْهَبٌ (madhab — school of law — literally "a going"), مَوْقِفٌ (mawqif — stance/position).',
