      answer: 'A conditional sentence consists of: (1) Adat al-shart (conditional particle): إِنْ (in — if), مَنْ (man — whoever), مَا (ma — whatever), مَهْمَا (mahma — whatever), مَتَى (mata — whenever), أَيْنَمَا (ayname — wherever), كُلَّمَا (kullama — whenever); (2) Fil al-shart (condition verb) — in jazm; (3) Jawab al-shart (consequence/response) — also in jazm. Example: إِنْ تَدْرُسْ تَنْجَحْ (if you study, you will succeed — both verbs in jazm). When the jawab is a nominal sentence or a verbal sentence beginning with a verb in the past tense, it requires a fa (فَ) or إِذَا: إِنْ جَاءَ زَيْدٌ فَأَكْرِمْهُ (if Zayd comes, then honor him — fa required because jawab is amr). The particle إِنْ (in) is the primary shart particle — others work similarly but indicate generality (man, ma, mahma) or time/place (mata, aynamna).',
