      id: 'n37',
      question: 'What is the Al-Mafuul lahu (المَفْعُول لَهُ/لِأَجْلِهِ — object of cause) and when is a preposition required instead?',
      answer: 'The Mafuul lahu is a masdar in nasb expressing the reason or motive for the main action. Conditions for the bare nasb: (1) Must be a masdar; (2) Must share the same subject as the main verb; (3) Must be simultaneous with the main action; (4) Must express the motive/cause. Example: قُمْتُ إِجْلَالًا لَكَ (I stood out of respect for you — ijlalan is in nasb). When any condition is not met, a preposition (li, min ajl, li-ajl) must be used: (a) Different subject: جِئْتُكَ لِإِكْرَامِكَ (I came so you would be honored — the honor is for you, not by you); (b) Not simultaneous: قَدِمْتُ مِنْ أَجْلِ العِلْمِ (I came for the sake of knowledge — knowledge acquisition is not simultaneous with traveling); (c) Not a masdar: جِئْتُ لِلتَّعَلُّمِ. This nuance distinguishes advanced students from beginners in parsing classical Arabic texts.',
