      answer: 'Ishtimghal occurs when a noun precedes a verb that is "preoccupied" with a pronoun referring to that noun, preventing the verb from directly governing the noun. Example: زَيْدًا ضَرَبْتُهُ (Zayd — I struck him). Analysis: the verb darabtu is preoccupied with the damir -hu (referring to Zayd) and cannot directly take Zayd as its maful. Zayd must be governed by an implied verb: ضَرَبْتُ زَيْدًا ضَرَبْتُهُ (I struck Zayd, I struck him). The fronted noun can be: (1) In nasb — if the implied verb is transitive (most common and preferred when the explicit verb is transitive); (2) In raf — if we analyze the fronted noun as a mubtada with the verbal sentence as khabar. Rules for which to prefer: (a) Nasb is preferred when the explicit verb is transitive; (b) Raf (mubtada analysis) is preferred when a particle requiring a mubtada follows, or when a conditional particle precedes.',
