      question: 'What is the Al-Tartib (التَّرْتِيب — order/sequence) in the coordination of sentences using atf particles?',
      answer: 'The atf particles encode different types of sequence and relationship between joined elements: (1) وَ (wa — and): no sequence implied — the two elements are joined without specifying which came first: جَاءَ زَيْدٌ وَعَمْرٌو (Zayd and Amr came — we don\'t know who came first); (2) فَ (fa — then/so): immediate sequence — the second follows directly after the first with no gap: دَخَلَ فَجَلَسَ (he entered and immediately sat); (3) ثُمَّ (thumma — then): delayed sequence — the second follows after a gap: دَخَلَ ثُمَّ جَلَسَ (he entered, then [later] sat); (4) حَتَّى (hatta — until/even): for gradation — the second is an extreme case of the first: قَدِمَ الحُجَّاجُ حَتَّى المَشَاةُ (the pilgrims arrived, even the ones on foot — the walkers are the extreme case); (5) أَوْ (aw): choice or uncertainty; (6) أَمْ (am): for questions of choice. Using the wrong particle changes the meaning: دَخَلَ وَجَلَسَ (he entered and sat) vs. دَخَلَ فَجَلَسَ (he entered then immediately sat) have different implications.',
