      answer: 'Qatr an-Nada wa Ball al-Sada (The Dewdrop and the Quenching of Thirst) by Ibn Hisham al-Ansari (d. 761 AH) is one of the most important intermediate Arabic grammar texts. It is a prose work covering: irab, the nominal and verbal sentence, the nawasikh, huruf al-jarr, tawabi, exceptional constructions, and hadhf. Its commentary (Sharh Qatr al-Nada — written by Ibn Hisham himself) is the primary teaching text. Position in the curriculum: (1) It comes after al-Ajurrumiyyah (beginner) and before the Alfiyyah (advanced); (2) It is the standard intermediate text in many traditional and modern Arabic programs; (3) Its companion text is Shudhur al-Dhahab (another Ibn Hisham text on syntax). Together with the Alfiyyah and Mughni al-Labib, Ibn Hisham\'s works constitute the three levels of classical Arabic grammar study: elementary (Qatr/Shudhur), comprehensive (Alfiyyah commentary), and reference (Mughni al-Labib). Ibn Hisham is known for his analytical clarity and his ability to resolve long-standing grammatical disputes.',
