      question: 'What is the Al-Muhkam wal-Mutashabih (المُحْكَم وَالمُتَشَابِه) in Quranic sciences?',
      answer: 'Al-Muhkam (clear) and al-Mutashabih (ambiguous/allegorical) are categories of Quranic verses established in Quran 3:7: "He it is who has sent down to you the Book; in it are verses that are muhkam — they are the mother of the Book — and others that are mutashabih." Muhkam: verses whose meaning is clear, unambiguous, and not open to multiple interpretations — form the foundation of the Quran\'s legislative content. Mutashabih: verses whose precise meaning is unclear, ambiguous, or allegorical — such as the opening letters (huruf muqattaat), verses describing attributes of Allah using words with physical connotations (يَدُ اللَّهِ — the hand of Allah; الاِسْتِوَاء عَلَى العَرْش), and verses on the precise timing of the Hour. Scholarly responses to mutashabih: (1) Tafwid — entrusting the meaning to Allah without interpretation (preferred by early scholars and the majority of Sunnis); (2) Tawil — reinterpreting metaphorically within proper bounds (approach of later Ashari scholars like al-Nawawi).',
